[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Basic MongoDB Shell Operations (CRUD)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial covers the fundamental CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations using the MongoDB shell. We'll explore how to interact with a MongoDB database and perform basic data manipulation."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Connecting to MongoDB"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before you begin, ensure that MongoDB is installed and running. Open your terminal or command prompt and type 'mongo' to connect to the MongoDB shell. If MongoDB is running on a different host or port, specify the connection string accordingly (e.g., 'mongo --host <hostname> --port <port>')."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"mongo"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Selecting a Database"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"To select a database, use the 'use' command followed by the database name. If the database doesn't exist, MongoDB will create it when you first store data in it."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"use mydatabase"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Create (Insert)"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"To insert a document into a collection, use the 'insertOne()' or 'insertMany()' methods. 'insertOne()' inserts a single document, while 'insertMany()' inserts multiple documents."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"// Insert one document
db.mycollection.insertOne( { item: "card", qty: 15 } )

// Insert multiple documents
db.mycollection.insertMany([
  { item: "envelope", qty: 20 },
  { item: "stamps", qty: 30 }
])"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Read (Find)"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"To retrieve documents from a collection, use the 'find()' method. You can specify a query to filter the results. To retrieve all documents, use 'find()' without any arguments."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"// Find all documents
db.mycollection.find()

// Find documents where qty is greater than 15
db.mycollection.find( { qty: { $gt: 15 } } )"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Update"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"To update documents, use the 'updateOne()' or 'updateMany()' methods. These methods require a query to identify the documents to update and an update document that specifies the changes to make. The '$set' operator is commonly used to update specific fields."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"// Update one document where item is "card"
db.mycollection.updateOne( { item: "card" }, { $set: { qty: 20 } } )

// Update all documents where qty is less than 25
db.mycollection.updateMany( { qty: { $lt: 25 } }, { $set: { status: "A" } } )"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Delete"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"To delete documents, use the 'deleteOne()' or 'deleteMany()' methods. These methods require a query to identify the documents to delete. To delete all documents in a collection, use an empty query ({})."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"// Delete one document where item is "stamps"
db.mycollection.deleteOne( { item: "stamps" } )

// Delete all documents where status is "A"
db.mycollection.deleteMany( { status: "A" } )"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Important Considerations"},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always use appropriate queries to target the correct documents for update and delete operations to avoid unintended data loss."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use the 'result' object returned by these methods to verify the success of the operation and the number of documents affected."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"For more complex queries and operations, refer to the official MongoDB documentation."},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial provides a basic introduction to CRUD operations in MongoDB. Mastering these operations is crucial for effectively managing data in your MongoDB databases."}]
